more and more parents are being  reassured by the measures that we are  taking to keep their children safe and  are letting them return to schools and  nurseries if they're eligible to do so  as of this week they're more than 1.6  million children back in our schools if  you're one of those parents I'd like to  say thank you for putting your trust in  us I know many of you have had doubts  and they still do I want you to know  that I treat my responsibilities for  your children with the utmost utmost  seriousness their safety their  well-being is my top priority  in welcoming more children back  we have followed not only the best  scientific advice but also what other  countries have been doing to manage a  successful return for their children and  making sure we do the same here in the  United Kingdom and my last briefing I  told you how it was my intention for all  children to be back in school for  September today I want to give you more  detail about how we're going to make  that happen but before I do I'd like  once more to thank the fantastic school  and college staff their child minders  the nursery workers and carers who are  all making a return possible schools  nurseries and earlier settings have been  welcoming more children back since the  1st of June they've been keeping  children in small consistent groups with  stringent infection control hygiene and  cleaning measures in place to make sure  that they're safe now that infection  rates are falling we can relax these  bubbles and from the 20th of July we can  lift restrictions on early years  settings who will be able to return to  their normal group sizes although other  safety measures must remain in place we  know that keeping contacts to a minimum  is still one of the best  ways to reduce risk of infection and  from this autumn when the whole school  population returns we're asking schools  to make sure that they continue to  minimize contacts this should be done by  distancing where possible and by keeping  classes or year groups separate from one  another we've suggested for instance  this schools may stagger they start and  end of a school day and that they keep  their corridors as free from congestion  as possible  minimizing movement of children we've  issued for guidance on the approach  schools and colleges should take and  they expect them to apply this in a way  that works most effectively for them  their students but also their wider  communities we know that maintaining  distance and bubbles may be more  challenging in schools where children  and young people have special  educational needs and disabilities so  we've issued specific guidance for them  if anyone in a nursery school or college  develops kovat 19 symptoms they can  already be tested but from September  we're going to give all schools and  colleges a limited number of home  testing kits to give to any children or  staff who are unable to get to a testing  center parents expect their children to  be safe in school but they also expect  them to be getting a first-class  education which is why from September  we'll be asking schools to resume a  broad and ambitious curriculum and we  expect exams to be going ahead in the  summer of 2021 we want children to be  getting back to normal and enjoying  school and benefiting from it we all  know that the best place for children to  learn is in school which is why I've  been so determined to get them back  there as soon as we can
 

